From 913d37fc472952e5bcafc7f47a59f018f8d382b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:29:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall: Egress and Forwards tabs, plus backup/restore

The Phase 2 and Phase 3 engines shipped in 5c13a9b3ac with no way to author
anything for them - both lists were nvram-only, and their record formats are
'>'-delimited strings whose malformed entries are silently DROPPED by design
(S2 never emits a half-formed rule). A control that can look set and do nothing
is the failure class this project keeps hunting, so hand-editing was never an
acceptable long-term answer.

EGRESS tab (reaper_fw_edef): pick a device object or group, choose Deny / Reject
/ Allow, optionally schedule it. The note says plainly that LAN traffic is never
affected, because "no internet for this device" and "cut this device off the
network" are very different requests and the -o $WANIF anchor only does the
first.

FORWARDS tab (reaper_fw_nat): protocol, external port, internal address and
port, an optional source restriction (any address object - including a geo
object, which resolves to Warden's country set) and an optional schedule. The
add handler mirrors the generator's own refusals - IPv4 literal required, no
comma list for the internal port - so an operator hears about it at the form
rather than finding a dropped record in syslog later.

BACKUP / RESTORE: a new read-only `export` CGI action returns the eight editable
lists. There is deliberately NO matching import action. Restore replays the
ordinary per-list `save`, so an imported configuration passes exactly the same
length and charset checks an interactive edit does; a bespoke import path would
be a second, less-travelled way into the same nvram, and the point of S2 is that
there is only one. Import lands in the DRAFT, so commit-confirm still gates it
going live.

RFW_204..RFW_223 seeded English into all 25 packs, lockstep at 6544 lines.

Verified without a build: every id the new JS touches exists in the markup; both
new tabs have a strip entry, a content div and a TABS[] entry; all page tokens
resolve against EN.dict; reaper_langcheck 0 breakers; 125 inline script blocks
across four reaper pages parse under node in all 25 languages; ASCII-clean.

STILL NOT BUILT from the Phase 2/3 plan, and neither is a wiring job:
 - Rule tracer. A config-walking simulator in C; a diagnostic aid rather than a
   control, so it is the least costly thing to defer.
 - dnsmasq ipset= for FQDN objects. BLOCKED, and not for the reason the plan
   assumed: it is not that dnsmasq.conf is regenerated wholesale (rc owns that
   file and can emit its own lines before the operator's hook) - it is that this
   dnsmasq is COMPILED WITHOUT ipset support. config.h defines both HAVE_IPSET
   and NO_IPSET and resolves it as `#if defined(NO_IPSET) -> #undef HAVE_IPSET`,
   and the shipped binary contains no ipset option string at all. Enabling it
   means rebuilding dnsmasq with HAVE_IPSET, which is a change to the most
   critical service on the box and wants its own rung and its own metal pass.
   FQDN objects continue to resolve on the 10-minute cru timer meanwhile.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
